IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cla-damages`
IMPORT `cla-cases-damages`
IMPORT `cla-qa`

-- Civil Liability Act 2002 (WA) -- cases for the Q&A layer (cla-qa.l4): every exported question, for every role and
-- every situation.
--
-- Each question is asked for the role it is not about, with every other input an ASSUMEd value that has none: the
-- answer must be `does not arise for you`, and reading any of those inputs would stop the evaluation with an error
-- ("it is an assumed term"). Each question that takes a situation input is asked, for its own role, with FALSE (the
-- situation does not hold) and every later input ASSUMEd: again `does not arise for you`. For the injured person (the
-- baseline's seat) the answers are worked by hand and also asserted equal to the baseline export's own answer on the
-- same facts. For the defendant they are worked by hand from the text.
--
-- The facts reuse the baseline's fixtures (cla-cases-damages.l4, imported for its definitions only: an imported
-- module's assertions are not run): Jake doored on a delivery (k044), with the baseline's contrast cases and
-- illustrative 2002-03 amounts (A $12 000, B $5 000, C $36 500; average weekly earnings $2 000 full-time adult and
-- $1 500 all employees, illustrative). Expected values are written by hand from the deposited text, never copied from
-- the output.
--
-- BASELINE. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md, "Q&A layer (seat and situations as parameters)".

§ `Cases -- the Q&A layer`

`as the injured person` MEANS `I was injured and am claiming damages`
`as the defendant` MEANS `A claim for damages for an injury is made against me`
`the amounts` MEANS `the 2002-03 amounts with illustrative earnings`
`the trail crash` MEANS `a mountain-bike crash on a commercial trail`
`downhill, obvious` MEANS `downhill mountain biking, an obvious risk of a fall`

§§ `For the injured person: the situation holds, and the answer is the baseline's`

-- ss 3A, 5A, 6.
#ASSERT `Which of Part 1A applies to the claim I make` `as the injured person` `Jake doored on a delivery, the CTP Act applying` EQUALS `the answer is` `all of Part 1A: duty, causation, recreational activities, contributory negligence, obvious and inherent risk (ss 5B-5P)`
#ASSERT `Which of Part 1A applies to the claim I make` `as the injured person` `a worker's common-law claim against the employer` EQUALS `the answer is` `none of Part 1A`
#ASSERT `Which of Part 1A applies to the claim I make` `as the injured person` `a smoker's claim against a tobacco company` EQUALS `the answer is` `only ss 5A-5D: the general duty and causation`
#ASSERT `Which of Part 1A applies to the claim I make` `as the injured person` `a fall in a shop in June 2003` EQUALS `the answer is` (`Which of Part 1A applies to my claim` `a fall in a shop in June 2003`)
#ASSERT `Which limits apply to the damages I claim` `as the injured person` `Jake doored on a delivery, the CTP Act applying` EQUALS `the answer is` `the limits in the Motor Vehicle (Third Party Insurance) Act 1943, not Part 2`
#ASSERT `Which limits apply to the damages I claim` `as the injured person` `Jake doored on a delivery, the door held not part of the driving` EQUALS `the answer is` `Part 2 of the Civil Liability Act: the general damages threshold, the earnings cap and the home care limits`
#ASSERT `Which limits apply to the damages I claim` `as the injured person` `an injury on a domestic flight` EQUALS `the answer is` `none of Part 2: carriage by air`
#ASSERT `Which limits apply to the damages I claim` `as the injured person` `a claim on a life insurance policy` EQUALS `the answer is` (`Which limits apply to my damages` `a claim on a life insurance policy`)

-- s 4: $12 420 rounds to $12 500; $37 230 to $37 000; $12 750 ($250 over) up to $13 000.
